    (
        1,
        35,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Gabriel&#x27;s &#x27;The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God&#x27; (Lk 1:35), Cyril reads the doubled-pneumatology (Spirit-comes-upon + Highest-overshadows) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Incarnation: not Adoption (the Spirit acts at conception, not at baptism), not Apollinarianism (the holy thing born IS the Son of God in his humanity), not Nestorianism (one subject is named, not two). The Tewahedo Theotokos doctrine cites Lk 1:35 as the canonical Lukan doubled-anchor with Mt 1:20 — the Spirit&#x27;s-overshadowing makes Mary the bearer of God-in-flesh, not merely the bearer of a man.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily II-III (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
    (
        1,
        35,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Epistulae ad Serapionem 1.6</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And the angel answered and said unto her, The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee: therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God&#x27; — Athanasius marks the ANNUNCIATION as the PRINCIPAL TRINITARIAN-INCARNATIONAL-MOMENT: the Spirit (Pneuma Hagion) comes-upon the Virgin to prepare-and-sanctify her flesh as worthy-temple; the dynamis tou Hypsistou (power of the Highest) is the divine-Logos who is conceived-in-her; the Hypsistos (the Highest = the Father) is the sending-source. All three Persons are active in the single Annunciation-event. The episkiasei (overshadow) verb echoes the Shekinah-cloud overshadowing the tabernacle (Exo 40:34-38 LXX) — Mary becomes the new tabernacle of the Word&#x27;s-glorious-presence. Tewahedo Marian-theology (Kidana Mahrat, the Covenant of Mercy) rests on this Annunciation-Trinitarian-overshadowing-Theotokos-ecclesia anchor.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Epistulae ad Serapionem de Spiritu Sancto (Letters to Serapion on the Holy Spirit, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857). The Serapion correspondence is Athanasius's principal anti-pneumatomachian work, refuting the Tropici denial of the Holy Spirit's divinity and homoousion with Father + Son.",
    ),
