    (
        1,
        38,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Mary&#x27;s &#x27;Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it unto me according to thy word&#x27; (Lk 1:38), Cyril reads the fiat-mihi as the canonical anchor of the doctrine that the Incarnation requires human consent — a free-and-deliberate Yes from the side of Mary, not coerced. The Virgin&#x27;s obedient response reverses Eve&#x27;s refusal (Tertullian&#x27;s typology, received by Cyril): Eve disbelieved and brought death, Mary believed and brought life. Tewahedo Marian piety cites Lk 1:38 as the canonical anchor of the doctrine that Mary is the New Eve whose deliberate Yes restores the human race to filial obedience — preserved in the Wǝddase Maryam refrain &#x27;Behold, the handmaid of the Lord.&#x27;</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily III (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
