        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>Christ&#x27;s reply to the devil — &#x27;Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve&#x27; — is for Cyril the canonical Adamic reversal: where the first Adam consented to the serpent&#x27;s offered elevation, the New Adam refuses every offered kingdom that does not come through the cross. The temptation is the wilderness-pendant of the Eden episode. Tewahedo Lenten lectionary places the Temptation narrative at the gateway of the Great Fast for precisely this Cyrillian reading.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily VII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
