        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s commission to Simon &#x27;Fear not; from henceforth thou shalt catch men&#x27; (Lk 5:10), Cyril reads the fishers-of-men image as the canonical Lukan anchor of the apostolic-vocation: Peter who failed at fishing all night succeeds at men-fishing under Christ&#x27;s word, foreshadowing the apostolic catch of three-thousand on the Day of Pentecost (Acts 2:41). The verb zōgrōn (capture-alive) is positive: the apostolic mission rescues, does not destroy. Tewahedo apostolic theology cites Lk 5:10 + Acts 2:41 as the canonical doubled-witness that the apostolic mission is the bringing-into-life of those caught by the gospel; the Tewahedo episcopate continues the apostolic catch.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXXV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Acts 2:41.",
