        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;putting forth his hand, [he] touched him, saying, I will: be thou clean&#x27; (Lk 5:13), Cyril reads the touch-of-the-leper as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the Incarnation reverses the contagion-pattern of impurity: the leper does NOT defile Christ, Christ cleanses the leper. Christ&#x27;s bodily-touch is sacramental — the Word-made-flesh transfers life and cleanness by physical contact. Tewahedo healing-rite theology cites Lk 5:13 as the canonical anchor for the bodily-touch component of the laying-on-of-hands healing rite and for the priestly anointing-with-oil (cf. James 5:14); the priest&#x27;s hand carries Christ&#x27;s cleansing.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXXVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. James 5:14.",
