        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance&#x27; (Lk 5:32), Cyril reads the mission-statement as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the Incarnation is medicinal-soteriological in character: Christ-the-Physician comes for the sick. The pairing with Lk 19:10 (&#x27;the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost&#x27;) frames the entire Lukan trajectory as a search-and-rescue mission. Tewahedo penance-theology cites Lk 5:32 as the canonical anchor of the doctrine that the priest&#x27;s call-to-confession follows the dominical pattern: not exclusion of sinners but invitation to repentance.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Lk 19:10.",
