    (
        7,
        47,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Her sins, which are many, are forgiven; for she loved much&#x27; — Cyril reads the saying not as forgiveness CAUSED by love but as forgiveness MANIFESTED by love. The woman&#x27;s tears at the Pharisee&#x27;s table are the visible token that her sins are already remitted; the love comes from the remission, the remission is not the wage of the love. Tewahedo penitential rite preserves this Cyrillian ordering: absolution precedes the works of penance, never the reverse.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily XL (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
    ),
    (
        7,
        48,
        "",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s direct absolution-formula &#x27;And he said unto her, Thy sins are forgiven&#x27; (Lk 7:48), Cyril reads the second-person performative declaration (apheōntai sou hai hamartiai) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the priestly absolution-formula: Christ-in-Person speaks forgiveness directly, paralleled by the Johannine commission to the apostles (Jn 20:23 — &#x27;Whose soever sins ye remit, they are remitted unto them&#x27;). Tewahedo penance-theology cites Lk 7:48 + Jn 20:23 as the canonical doubled-witness for the priestly absolution: the priest declares &#x27;thy sins are forgiven&#x27; in persona Christi, exercising the apostolic commission.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 20:23.",
    ),
