        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s interpretation of the Sower parable &#x27;Now the parable is this: The seed is the word of God&#x27; (Lk 8:11), Cyril reads the seed = word-of-God identification as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the dynamic Word: the Word that creates (Gen 1) is the same Word that saves (the Gospel) and the same Word that perseveres (the canonical Scripture). The Word is seed because it carries its own life within itself. Tewahedo Bible-doctrine cites Lk 8:11 as the canonical Lukan anchor for the doctrine of Scripture-as-living-seed; the Tewahedo manuscript tradition&#x27;s reverence for the canonical word as bearing-its-own-life is rooted here.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
