        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the description of the freed Gerasene demoniac &#x27;[they] found the man, out of whom the devils were departed, sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed, and in his right mind&#x27; (Lk 8:35), Cyril reads the threefold-restoration (sitting-at-feet + clothed + right-mind) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Christian sanity as the restoration of catechetical posture: the freed soul takes its rightful disciple-position at the feet of the Lord, with the moral garments of virtue and the rational integration of right-mindedness. Tewahedo catechumenate cites Lk 8:35 as the canonical anchor for the catechumen&#x27;s restored posture — at Christ&#x27;s feet, clothed in the white baptismal garment, sound of mind.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
