        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Daughter, be of good comfort: thy faith hath made thee whole; go in peace&#x27; (Lk 8:48), Cyril reads the second instance of the Lukan &#x27;thy-faith-hath-saved-thee&#x27; formula (after Lk 7:50) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the woman&#x27;s bold-touch-in-faith is the active-receptive cause of her healing: she did not need permission, the touch of faith drew Christ&#x27;s power. Tewahedo theology of bold-faith cites Lk 8:48 as the canonical anchor of the doctrine that the believing approach to the consecrated species (the body and blood) is the parallel of the hemorrhaging woman&#x27;s healing-touch; one approaches the Eucharist in the same posture.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
