        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s call to Jairus&#x27;s daughter &#x27;Maid, arise&#x27; (Lk 8:54), Cyril reads the imperative-command-as-resurrection-word as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that Christ&#x27;s word effects what it says: the same authority that &#x27;in the beginning was the Word&#x27; (Jn 1:1) speaks the dead to life with a single imperative. The doubled-raising of Lk 7 (Nain) + Lk 8 (Jairus&#x27;s daughter) prepares the reader for Lk 24 (Christ&#x27;s own resurrection): Christ raises others by his word, then is raised himself by the Father. Tewahedo Fasika theology cites Lk 8:54 + Jn 11:43 + Lk 24:46 as the canonical triple-witness of the word-effects-resurrection power that culminates in Christ&#x27;s own raising.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 11:43, Lk 24:46.",
