        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>The Lukan Pater Noster — &#x27;When ye pray, say, Our Father which art in heaven&#x27; — is for Cyril the canonical form by which the Word teaches the saints to address God in his own filial voice. The &#x27;Our&#x27; is ecclesial: no one prays the prayer alone; even the hermit in the desert prays as a member of the body. Tewahedo daily-office incorporates the Pater Noster at every hour, drawing directly on Cyril&#x27;s reading of it as the canonical-filial address form.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
