        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;But if I with the finger of God cast out devils&#x27; (Lk 11:20), Cyril reads the distinctive-Lukan &#x27;finger of God&#x27; (where Mt 12:28 has &#x27;Spirit of God&#x27;) as the canonical Lukan anchor that links Christ&#x27;s exorcisms to the Exodus plague-narratives — the Egyptian magicians confessed &#x27;this is the finger of God&#x27; (Exo 8:19). The Mosaic &#x27;finger&#x27; = the Spirit, the same Spirit that wrote the Decalogue on stone tablets (Exo 31:18). Tewahedo christology cites Lk 11:20 + Exo 8:19 + Exo 31:18 + Mt 12:28 as the canonical fourfold-witness that the Mosaic-finger-of-God is identified with the Spirit-of-God operative in Christ&#x27;s ministry; the same Spirit is the divine agent of liberation and of canon-formation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Mt 12:28, Exo 8:19, Exo 31:18.",
