        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee&#x27; — the rich fool&#x27;s death-warrant in the parable is for Cyril the canonical warning against the postponement-of-the-soul that worldly wealth enables. The man addresses HIS OWN SOUL (&#x27;soul, thou hast much goods&#x27;) as if it belonged to him to dispose; God&#x27;s reply discloses that the soul is on lease. Tewahedo monastic memento-mori catechesis draws directly on this Cyrillian reading.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
