    (
        12,
        32,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Fear not, little flock; for it is your Father&#x27;s good pleasure to give you the kingdom&#x27; (Lk 12:32), Cyril reads the doubled-tenderness (little flock + Father&#x27;s good pleasure) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the Church&#x27;s smallness is no obstacle to the Kingdom&#x27;s gift: God&#x27;s good pleasure (eudokia — the same word as &#x27;good will toward men&#x27; in the angelic hymn Lk 2:14) does not measure recipients by their numbers. Tewahedo ecclesiology, mindful of the minority-position of the Ethiopian Tewahedo Church in the broader Christian world, cites Lk 12:32 as the canonical anchor of the doctrine that the small flock of right-believers (the Tewahedo &#x27;right-believing&#x27; identity) receives the same kingdom as any larger communion.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily LXXXIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
