        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the parable&#x27;s opening &#x27;A certain man had two sons&#x27; (Lk 15:11), Cyril reads the two-sons frame as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the parable&#x27;s THIRD pivot (after shepherd + housekeeper) gives the Father his speaking-part: the father whose first speech is the silent embrace at 15:20, whose second speech is the celebration-command, whose third is the explanation to the elder brother. The full-pericope (Lk 15:11-32) is Christ&#x27;s most-extended-character-development parable; the father&#x27;s character is most-fully-disclosed. Tewahedo pastoral theology cites Lk 15:11-32 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the Father&#x27;s threefold-speech-of-mercy — the priest who absolves speaks as the father in the parable speaks.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CIX (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
