        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;When he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him&#x27; — Cyril treats the Father&#x27;s running as the canonical signature of the Lukan parable: the divine compassion does not wait for the sinner to arrive but goes out to meet him while he is still far. The first robe, the ring, and the shoes are the baptismal restoration of dignity. Tewahedo penitential theology cites Lk 15:20-22 as the principal canonical anchor of God-runs-to-meet-the-returning-sinner imagery.</p></aside>',
