        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you, say, We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do&#x27; (Lk 17:10), Cyril reads the unprofitable-servants-formula as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of grace-against-merit: even maximal Christian-virtuous-action puts the disciple no further than baseline-duty; salvation is never owed to the disciple. The Greek achreioi (unprofitable, but also &#x27;without-superfluous-claim&#x27;) captures the disposition of the just-debtor with no surplus. Tewahedo monastic theology cites Lk 17:10 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the monk&#x27;s perpetual-humility-formula — &#x27;I am an unprofitable servant&#x27; is recited in the daily-office.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXVIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
