        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s command &#x27;Suffer little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God&#x27; (Lk 18:16), Cyril reads the doubled-imperative (suffer-them + forbid-them-not) as the canonical Lukan anchor of paedo-receptivity and (in the wider patristic tradition) infant-baptism: children are not deferred until adult-comprehension but received-now into the kingdom. The kingdom belongs not to children alone but to those characterized by child-like receptivity (Lk 18:17). Tewahedo baptismal theology — practising infant-baptism (often on the 40th day for boys, 80th day for girls per Lev 12 + Lk 2:22-24) — cites Lk 18:16 as the canonical Lukan-NT anchor for the practice.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXIII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Lev 12, Lk 2:22-24.",
