    (
        19,
        5,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s call to Zacchaeus &#x27;make haste, and come down; for to day I must abide at thy house&#x27; (Lk 19:5), Cyril reads the dei-me (it-is-necessary-for-me) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of Christ&#x27;s seeking-grace: the dominical &#x27;I must lodge with thee&#x27; is a divine-imperative, not a guest&#x27;s-request. Christ initiates the encounter; Zacchaeus&#x27;s tree-climbing met halfway by Christ&#x27;s calling-down. Tewahedo evangelism-theology cites Lk 19:5 + Lk 19:10 (Son of Man come to seek-and-save) as the canonical Lukan doubled-witness of the doctrine that Christ initiates the salvation-encounter; the priestly-pastor follows the dominical-pattern of seeking-the-Zacchaeuses.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
    ),
