        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;This day is salvation come to this house&#x27; — Cyril reads the Zacchaeus pericope as the canonical Lukan diptych to Lk 15: in Lk 15 the lost son returns to the father; in Lk 19 the lost son is sought-and-found by the Father. The Son of Man came TO SEEK AND TO SAVE — the seeking is the divine initiative. Tewahedo missiology cites Lk 19:10 alongside Lk 15:20 as the canonical seeking-and-running diptych.</p></aside>',
