        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s mission-statement at the Zacchaeus episode &#x27;For the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost&#x27; (Lk 19:10), Cyril reads the doubled-mission (seek + save) as the canonical Lukan summary-statement of the Incarnation&#x27;s purpose: the Son of Man&#x27;s earthly trajectory (already-emphasized at Lk 5:32 — come to call sinners) reaches its compact-definitive expression here. The verse becomes load-bearing for the patristic doctrine of the missio Dei — God&#x27;s outgoing mission preceding any human response. Tewahedo missionary theology cites Lk 19:10 + Lk 5:32 as the canonical Lukan doubled-witness of the doctrine that Christian mission participates in the dominical-search-and-rescue mission; the Tewahedo apostolic-outreach to the Beta-Israel and surrounding peoples is the appropriation of this canonical pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXXVII (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Lk 5:32.",
