        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On Christ&#x27;s &#x27;For he is not a God of the dead, but of the living: for all live unto him&#x27; (Lk 20:38), Cyril reads the all-live-unto-him (pantes gar autō zōsin — Luke&#x27;s distinctive addition over Mark/Matthew) as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine that the patriarchs whom God identifies himself with (Abraham, Isaac, Jacob) are alive-to-God right now — not merely scheduled-for-future-resurrection. The intermediate-state of the righteous is conscious-life-with-God. Tewahedo intermediate-state-theology cites Lk 20:38 as the canonical Lukan anchor doubled with Lk 16:23 (Lazarus in Abraham&#x27;s bosom) and Lk 23:43 (today thou shalt be with me in paradise) — the conscious-life-with-Christ of the departed righteous is canonically witnessed three-times in Luke alone.</p></aside>',
