        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;This is my body which is given for you: this do in remembrance of me&#x27; — Cyril treats the Lukan institution narrative as the canonical anchor of his entire eucharistic theology: that the bread broken IS the body of Christ, that the body of Christ given for the believers is the same body born of the Virgin, that the Eucharist is the ongoing communication of the life-giving flesh of the Word. Tewahedo Anaphora cites Lk 22:19 as one of its principal canonical warrants of the real presence.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLI (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
