        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the disciples&#x27; Last-Supper strife — &#x27;there was also a strife among them, which of them should be accounted the greatest&#x27; (Lk 22:24) — Cyril reads the strife-at-the-Last-Supper as the canonical Lukan anti-anchor: the very moment of institution, when the Word made flesh is on the verge of his death, the disciples argue about greatness. Christ&#x27;s response (22:26-27 — &#x27;I am among you as he that serveth&#x27;) makes priestly-service the inversion of secular-greatness. Tewahedo episcopal-formation cites Lk 22:24-27 + Jn 13:14 (foot-washing) as the canonical doubled-witness for the doctrine of the bishop-as-servant — the highest ecclesial office is the lowest service-vocation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLIV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 13:14.",
