        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;His sweat was as it were great drops of blood falling down to the ground&#x27; — Cyril treats the Gethsemane sweat as the canonical anchor of the Lord&#x27;s true humanity: the agony is real, the prayer is real, the consenting will is real. Against any Apollinarian reduction of the human will, Cyril insists that the Word in his humanity wills our salvation through real prayer to the Father. Tewahedo Holy-Thursday lectionary preserves Lk 22:44 as the canonical anchor of the Lord&#x27;s prayerful humanity.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CXLVI (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
