    (
        23,
        33,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the place-name &#x27;Calvary&#x27; (Lk 23:33 — kranion &#x27;place of a skull&#x27;), Cyril reads the Hebrew-Aramaic Golgotha as the canonical Lukan anchor of the typological-doctrine that the place of Christ&#x27;s death is the symbolic place of Adam&#x27;s skull: the Adamic-tradition (preserved in Tewahedo Synaxarium and Mäshafä-Adam) locates Adam&#x27;s burial at Golgotha, making Christ&#x27;s blood flow over the first father&#x27;s bones. Tewahedo iconography cites Lk 23:33 + the Mäshafä-Adam tradition as the canonical doubled-witness for the depictive-tradition of the Adamic-skull at the foot of the Cross — the Cross stands over the head of the first father; the Second Adam restores what the First Adam lost.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Tewahedo apocryphal-tradition: Mäshafä Adam (Book of Adam).",
    ),
