        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit&#x27; — Cyril treats the Lukan death-cry, drawn from Ps 31:5, as the canonical anchor of every Christian&#x27;s death-bed prayer: the soul is commended to the Father who created it, by the same words the eternal Son used. The Word does not lose his divinity at the cry of dereliction; the soul-Father committal is the calm answer to the Markan-Matthean cry. Tewahedo funeral office cites Lk 23:46 as the canonical commendation-prayer.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLII (R. Payne Smith trans., Oxford: University Press, 1859). PD.",
