        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the Emmaus-road encounter (Lk 24:13-32), Cyril reads the unique-Lukan post-resurrection narrative as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the eucharistic-recognition pattern: word-of-Christ (expounded along the road, Lk 24:27) → breaking-of-bread (eyes opened, Lk 24:30-31 seed) → burning-hearts (in retrospect, Lk 24:32). The structure preserves the canonical Eucharistic shape: Liturgy of the Word + Liturgy of the Sacrament + Mystagogical reflection. Tewahedo Anaphora structure cites Lk 24:13-32 as the canonical Lukan anchor of the Eucharistic-recognition-shape; every Tewahedo Mass is an Emmaus-road journey.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLV (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD.",
