        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;He took bread, and blessed it, and brake, and gave to them. And their eyes were opened, and they knew him&#x27; — Cyril reads the Emmaus recognition as the canonical Lukan disclosure that the Risen Christ is known IN the breaking of the bread. The same gesture that instituted the Eucharist on Holy Thursday opens the disciples&#x27; eyes on Easter evening. Tewahedo eucharistic theology cites Lk 24:30-31 as the canonical anchor that every Eucharist is an Emmaus-recognition.</p></aside>',
