        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>On the risen Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Behold my hands and my feet, that it is I myself: handle me, and see; for a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as ye see me have&#x27; (Lk 24:39), Cyril reads the handle-me + flesh-and-bones formula as the canonical Lukan anchor of the doctrine of the real-bodily-resurrection: not a ghost, not a mere appearance, but a transformed-yet-real body with flesh-and-bones. The verse is load-bearing against the docetic-spiritualizing tendencies that would later challenge the resurrection. Tewahedo Easter theology cites Lk 24:39 + Jn 20:27 (&#x27;reach hither thy finger&#x27;) + 1 Cor 15:42-44 (resurrection body) as the canonical triple-witness for the real-bodily-resurrection doctrine; the Tewahedo Fasika Anaphora confesses &#x27;we believe in the resurrection of the body&#x27;.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke, Homily CLVI (R. Payne Smith trans., 1859). PD. Cf. Jn 20:27, 1 Cor 15:42-44.",
