        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Luke</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;He was parted from them, and carried up into heaven&#x27; — Cyril treats the Lukan Ascension as the canonical inauguration of the Word-made-flesh&#x27;s session at the Father&#x27;s right hand: the same humanity assumed from the Virgin is enthroned in glory. The Ascension is not the departure of the Word from his flesh but the elevation of the flesh into the divine glory. Tewahedo Ascension feast (Ǝrgät) cites Lk 24:51 alongside Acts 1:9-11 as the canonical diptych of the heavenly enthronement.</p></aside>',
